From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] ntdll: NtCreatePrivateNamespace, NtOpenPrivateNamespace, NtDeletePrivateNamespace.

The native calls take no name: the boundary descriptor is the namespace's identity
(its name and SIDs), and objects go in it by RootDirectory = the namespace handle,
as System Informer's PhCreatePrivateNamespace uses them. A namespace is an object
directory named after the descriptor in the session's BaseNamedObjects, so every
process with an equal descriptor reaches the same one, and it lives while handles to
it are open. The boundary is checked against the caller's token as kernelbase's
CreatePrivateNamespace does: the SIDs must be the token's user or enabled groups, the
integrity label at most the token's. System Informer's setup ended with
"unimplemented function ntdll.dll.NtOpenPrivateNamespace" after 7900.
---
